# Trees. A library of them, not a clip: files whose name starts with `_` are included and are
# never baked on their own, and this one contributes no geometry and paints nothing by itself —
# like `facility/_order.clip` it is a dictionary of parts, each drawn once about x = z = 0 and
# standing on y = 0, for a caller to `translate` into place.
#
# ================================================================================================
# WHY IT EXISTS
# ================================================================================================
#
# Because a tree was a cylinder with a ball on it. `estate/orangery.clip` read
#
#     let or_tree_leaf = sphere 0 2.79 0 r=0.855
#
# and twenty-one of those stood down the hall — one sphere of leaves each, all the same, all
# perfectly round. That reads as a lollipop at any distance, and it reads as a lollipop at 3.1 cm
# voxels, where there is enough resolution to see that it is wrong and nothing there to see.
#
# What a tree actually is, and what each of these is built out of, in the order it is built:
#
#   1. A TAPERING, WANDERING STEM. Five capsules, each starting where the last ended, the radius
#      stepping 0.135 -> 0.082 over 1.42 m. The step between two of them is 0.011, a third of a
#      voxel at metre 32, so the taper reads as continuous rather than as a stack of drums. It
#      also LEANS, and the lean changes: the azimuth turns 44 degrees a segment and the tilt runs
#      9, 7.5, 3.5, 5, 8 degrees, so the stem sweeps out to 70 mm and comes back under the fork.
#      A trunk drawn as one cylinder is a post. This one is a tree that has spent thirty years
#      reaching for a window.
#
#   2. A SECTION THAT IS NOT A CIRCLE. `displace { bole tree_bark } amount=0.032` — an fbm at
#      0.22 m, so the stem swells and hollows by +-0.032 (a whole voxel at metre 32) over a
#      distance of a few handspans. That is what makes the section lobed rather than round, and it
#      is the only thing here that can do it: a capsule is exactly circular by definition.
#
#      ONLY THE BOLE IS DISPLACED, and that is not laziness. A displacement applies to the whole
#      field under it, and 0.032 m of it into a secondary branch of radius 0.019 ERASES THE BRANCH
#      wherever the noise is negative — which does not fail, it silently drops lengths of the tree
#      and leaves the foliage they carried floating. So the wood is split at r = 0.048: thick
#      enough to carry the bark, or left alone. It is also the ONE displacement the tree keeps —
#      see the budget below — and it keeps it because the bole is five capsules in a box the size
#      of a bucket, so the slack it forces on the sampler is nowhere near anything.
#
#   3. PRIMARIES AND SECONDARIES. Five limbs (four on `citrus_b`) leaving the fork at tilts of 20
#      to 64 degrees from the vertical, at azimuths that are NOT evenly spaced — 16, 78, 140, 206,
#      312 — each of a different length and weight, so no view of the tree is the view a quarter
#      turn round. Each divides into two or three secondaries fanned 46 degrees either side of its
#      parent, and every limb sheds 13 degrees of tilt per metre as it goes, which is a branch
#      turning up towards the light. The radii thin 0.045 at the fork to 0.037 at the first
#      division to 0.024 and 0.016 at the ends — half a voxel at metre 32, which is deliberate:
#      the last twig is meant to be a hint inside the leaves and not a dowel.
#
#   4. FOLIAGE AS MANY CLUMPS, AND HOW THEY ARE GUARANTEED TO BE ONE PIECE. Thirty-odd ellipsoids
#      walked along the limbs from the fork outward. THE WALK IS THE WHOLE TRICK: the step from
#      one clump to the next is (r_here + r_next - 0.140), so consecutive clumps overlap by 140 mm
#      BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by luck, and `rr` is the SMALLEST semi-axis of each ellipsoid
#      with the other two only ever growing from it, so the guarantee holds in every direction.
#
#      This matters more than it sounds. A crown of clumps that merely look as though they touch
#      comes back from the sampler as thirty floating pieces — the failure `clipcheck` reports as
#      `components 31`, and which no picture of the field would ever show. 140 mm is over a whole
#      voxel at metre 8, four at metre 16 and four and a half at metre 32, so it survives every
#      pitch this repository samples at. All three were checked before a line of this was written.
#
#      The clumps are NOT a shell round a sphere. They sit on the limbs, the limbs radiate, and
#      the middle of the crown is left open — so the gaps are the wedges of air between one spray
#      and the next, which is where light actually gets through a tree, and the silhouette is the
#      outline of thirty different ellipsoids rather than the outline of one.
#
#   5. AND NOT A LEAF DISPLACEMENT ON TOP, WHICH IS THE ONE THING HERE THAT WAS BUILT, MEASURED
#      AND THEN TAKEN OUT AGAIN. `tree_leaf` below is still declared and still works, and a caller
#      with three trees rather than twenty-one should use it. The shipped trees do not, because on
#      a crown already made of thirty-seven clumps it bought 2.7% more surface for 50% more
#      sampler, and the budget for this was twice the old sphere. The whole table is under
#      `tree_leaf`. What it would have added is a bobble of about one voxel; what the clumps
#      already give is a silhouette made of thirty-seven different ellipsoids.
#
# ================================================================================================
# WHAT IT COSTS, AND WHY IT IS SHAPED THE WAY IT IS
# ================================================================================================
#
# `citrus_a` is 29 capsules, 37 ellipsoids and 11 spheres; `citrus_b` is 28, 36 and 9. That is a
# great deal beside a sphere, and it is affordable for one reason: THE FIELD IS A GRAPH, so a tree
# named by twenty-one places in a clip is one subtree asked about twenty-one boxes and not
# twenty-one trees. Every part carries a tight box, so the sampler only asks about a crown near a
# crown.
#
# THE BUDGET WAS TWICE THE OLD SPHERE AND THAT IS WHAT SET THE SHAPE OF THIS FILE. Marginal cost
# of ONE tree in its own box at metre 32, over the same box empty, everything interleaved in one
# run so that the machine's mood cancels out of the comparison, minimum of nine rounds:
#
#     the old cylinder, sphere and two rings of fruit    0.292 s     1.00x
#     citrus_a, as it ships                              0.624 s     2.14x
#     citrus_b, as it ships                              0.579 s     1.99x
#     citrus_a with a leaf displacement at 0.022         + 0.09      2.4x
#     citrus_a with a leaf displacement at 0.040         + 0.24      2.9x
#
# THAT IS A NUMBER WITH A WIDE ERROR BAR. This machine is shared, and citrus_a measured between
# 1.76x and 2.14x over four separate runs of the same comparison; the last two rows are ratios
# from a different run and are quoted as increments for that reason. What does not move between
# runs is the ORDER, and the order is the whole finding: the solids cost about twice the sphere
# and any leaf displacement on top costs about as much again.
#
# Two things were cut to get inside the budget and both are worth knowing. The secondaries were
# built as two capsules each and are now one: at 0.024 m of radius the kink between them was a
# quarter of a voxel and it cost fourteen solids a tree. And the leaf displacement went, for the
# reason written in 5 above.
#
# On the whole of `estate/orangery.clip` at metre 8, which has twenty-one of these in it, the
# change does not show at all: minima of five interleaved rounds gave 12.29 s of old trees against
# 13.22 s of new on a quiet machine and 38.00 against 35.08 on a loaded one — the sign of the
# difference is not even stable. Twenty-one crowns are about a thousandth of that box.
#
# ================================================================================================
# WHAT A CALLER GETS
# ================================================================================================
#
#   citrus_a_wood   citrus_a_leaf   citrus_a_fruit   citrus_a
#   citrus_b_wood   citrus_b_leaf   citrus_b_fruit   citrus_b
#
# Three parts each, because a tree is three materials and a paint rule has to name a shape that is
# only the thing it paints — plus the whole, for the solid. Bark on the wood, a leaf green on the
# foliage, the fruit's own colour on the fruit, and THE FRUIT RULE LAST: each orange is set 105 mm
# into the clump that carries it with 65 mm standing proud, so the rule that paints it has to win
# over the rule that paints the leaves.
#
# Both stand on y = 0, with the root a further 0.10 m below it so that a caller can bury the foot
# in whatever it grows out of:
#
#     let my_tree = translate { citrus_a } 0 0.675 0
#
#   citrus_a   3.19 m over the root, 3.09 above the ground line, head 1.69 x 1.56 m, eleven
#              oranges. 29 capsules, 37 clumps. The taller and more upright of the two; its
#              longest limb leans towards 140 degrees and its crown reaches furthest that way.
#   citrus_b   2.85 m over the root, 2.75 above it, head 1.50 x 1.53 m, nine oranges. 28 capsules,
#              36 clumps. Squatter, four primaries where A has five, sweeping the other way. It
#              exists so that a row of these is a row of TREES rather than a row of copies, which
#              is the other half of what was wrong with the sphere.
#
# Those boxes are measured, not intended: `clipcheck` on each tree alone at metre 32, 16 and 8,
# one component and nothing floating at all three.
#
# They are the same species and the same pruning — a citrus in a tub, a clear stem to about 1.3 m
# with a rounded head on it, which is what an orangery grows and is not a forest tree. Anything
# that wants a forest tree should add a third entry here rather than stretch one of these.

# ================================================================================================
# THE TWO GRAINS
# ================================================================================================
#
# `tree_bark` is a plain fbm, and it can be, because the bole it is applied to is 0.17 m thick at
# the root and nothing 0.032 m deep can cut that in half.
#
# `tree_leaf` IS DECLARED, WORKS, AND IS NOT USED BY EITHER TREE. That is the honest state of it
# and it is left here rather than deleted because it is right for a courtyard with three trees in
# it and wrong for a hall with twenty-one, and because what it cost is worth the next person's
# twenty minutes. Two things were settled about it and neither is obvious.
#
# THE SIGN, AND `low=0 high=-1` IS NOT A TYPO. A displacement that can be POSITIVE moves the
# surface INWARD, and inward on a leaf grain a few voxels across shears the caps off a crown made
# of small clumps. Measured on `citrus_a` alone at metre 32, everything else held:
#
#     `cells` at 0.105 remapped to +-0.75, amount 0.045    56 components, 107 voxels floating
#     the same field pushed OUTWARD only                    1 component
#     no leaf grain at all                                  1 component
#
# Fifty-six pieces of tree, none bigger than six voxels, every one of them in the crown, and not
# one of them visible in any number except `components`. Clamped to 0 .. -1 the field can bud a
# bobble of leaves off the surface and can never thin a neck, so the count cannot go up. `rasp`
# was tried too, and it is cheaper than either: it leaves four loose blobs in the air beside the
# crown, because a field that is large AWAY from a surface grows an island out there.
#
# THE PATTERN, WHICH IS WHERE THE COST IS. The sampler cannot skip as far near a displaced surface
# — the reach is charged twice, once for reading the field and once for stepping through it — and
# every voxel of that widened shell then has to evaluate the pattern. Split, on one tree at metre
# 32: a displacement of a CONSTANT, so the slack is paid and the pattern is free, costs +0.255 s;
# the two-octave fbm on top of that costs another +0.319; `cells` in its place costs another
# +0.45. `cells` is the better grain — it returns the distance to the nearest scattered point, so
# it has a CREASE where two cells meet, and a crease is what the edge of a leaf is — and it costs
# 81 hashes a sample against a single value-noise octave's 8. It is not worth it for a surface a
# few per cent rougher, so what is below is one octave of value noise, remapped outward.

let tree_bark = fbm size=0.22 octaves=3 seed=61
let tree_leaf = remap { noise size=0.16 seed=83 } from=-0.18 to=0.82 low=0 high=-1

# ================================================================================================
# CITRUS A — the taller of the two: five primaries, eleven oranges
# ================================================================================================

# The bole: the stem and the first reach of every primary, the wood thick enough that
# ct_a can carry a bark displacement without the displacement eating it.
let ct_a_s00 = capsule   0.000 -0.100   0.000     0.040  0.181   0.019  r=0.130
let ct_a_s01 = capsule   0.040  0.181   0.019     0.053  0.462   0.054  r=0.119
let ct_a_s02 = capsule   0.053  0.462   0.054     0.046  0.746   0.070  r=0.109
let ct_a_s03 = capsule   0.046  0.746   0.070     0.023  1.028   0.079  r=0.098
let ct_a_s04 = capsule   0.023  1.028   0.079    -0.014  1.310   0.065  r=0.087
let ct_a_bole = union {
    ct_a_s00 ct_a_s01 ct_a_s02
    ct_a_s03 ct_a_s04
}

# The branches: primaries thinning into secondaries. NOT displaced -- see the header.
let ct_a_b00 = capsule  -0.014  1.310   0.065     0.169  1.615   0.117  r=0.045
let ct_a_b01 = capsule   0.169  1.615   0.117     0.323  1.940   0.138  r=0.037
let ct_a_b02 = capsule   0.323  1.940   0.138     0.350  2.377   0.100  r=0.024
let ct_a_b03 = capsule   0.323  1.940   0.138     0.524  2.449   0.107  r=0.024
let ct_a_b04 = capsule   0.323  1.940   0.138     0.520  2.324   0.338  r=0.024
let ct_a_b05 = capsule  -0.014  1.310   0.065     0.031  1.415   0.276  r=0.035
let ct_a_b06 = capsule   0.031  1.415   0.276     0.116  1.525   0.471  r=0.029
let ct_a_b07 = capsule   0.116  1.525   0.471     0.316  1.754   0.582  r=0.019
let ct_a_b08 = capsule   0.116  1.525   0.471     0.225  1.697   0.724  r=0.019
let ct_a_b09 = capsule  -0.014  1.310   0.065    -0.124  1.704   0.157  r=0.043
let ct_a_b10 = capsule  -0.124  1.704   0.157    -0.235  2.103   0.228  r=0.035
let ct_a_b11 = capsule  -0.235  2.103   0.228    -0.231  2.803   0.302  r=0.023
let ct_a_b12 = capsule  -0.235  2.103   0.228    -0.299  2.756   0.261  r=0.023
let ct_a_b13 = capsule  -0.235  2.103   0.228    -0.489  2.624   0.153  r=0.023
let ct_a_b14 = capsule  -0.014  1.310   0.065    -0.205  1.476  -0.029  r=0.039
let ct_a_b15 = capsule  -0.205  1.476  -0.029    -0.373  1.649  -0.150  r=0.032
let ct_a_b16 = capsule  -0.373  1.649  -0.150    -0.567  1.919  -0.177  r=0.020
let ct_a_b17 = capsule  -0.373  1.649  -0.150    -0.588  1.883  -0.347  r=0.020
let ct_a_b18 = capsule  -0.373  1.649  -0.150    -0.448  1.809  -0.467  r=0.020
let ct_a_b19 = capsule  -0.014  1.310   0.065     0.130  1.533  -0.095  r=0.041
let ct_a_b20 = capsule   0.130  1.533  -0.095     0.241  1.772  -0.258  r=0.033
let ct_a_b21 = capsule   0.241  1.772  -0.258     0.234  2.243  -0.362  r=0.021
let ct_a_b22 = capsule   0.241  1.772  -0.258     0.400  2.101  -0.369  r=0.021
let ct_a_b23 = capsule   0.241  1.772  -0.258     0.491  2.129  -0.208  r=0.021
let ct_a_branches = union {
    ct_a_b00 ct_a_b01 ct_a_b02
    ct_a_b03 ct_a_b04 ct_a_b05
    ct_a_b06 ct_a_b07 ct_a_b08
    ct_a_b09 ct_a_b10 ct_a_b11
    ct_a_b12 ct_a_b13 ct_a_b14
    ct_a_b15 ct_a_b16 ct_a_b17
    ct_a_b18 ct_a_b19 ct_a_b20
    ct_a_b21 ct_a_b22 ct_a_b23
}

# The foliage: one clump per step along every limb from the fork outward.
let ct_a_c00 = ellipsoid  -0.007  1.461   0.032  rx=0.355 ry=0.328 rz=0.355
let ct_a_c01 = ellipsoid   0.127  1.536   0.107  rx=0.256 ry=0.254 rz=0.283
let ct_a_c02 = ellipsoid   0.259  1.836   0.144  rx=0.213 ry=0.207 rz=0.219
let ct_a_c03 = ellipsoid   0.326  1.936   0.157  rx=0.281 ry=0.277 rz=0.339
let ct_a_c04 = ellipsoid   0.346  2.269   0.105  rx=0.216 ry=0.189 rz=0.192
let ct_a_c05 = ellipsoid   0.357  2.374   0.093  rx=0.208 ry=0.180 rz=0.201
let ct_a_c06 = ellipsoid   0.467  2.278   0.110  rx=0.272 ry=0.216 rz=0.223
let ct_a_c07 = ellipsoid   0.510  2.449   0.089  rx=0.272 ry=0.232 rz=0.278
let ct_a_c08 = ellipsoid   0.469  2.242   0.306  rx=0.248 ry=0.221 rz=0.259
let ct_a_c09 = ellipsoid   0.526  2.344   0.351  rx=0.243 ry=0.227 rz=0.249
let ct_a_c10 = ellipsoid   0.019  1.433   0.274  rx=0.237 ry=0.223 rz=0.246
let ct_a_c11 = ellipsoid   0.114  1.527   0.486  rx=0.252 ry=0.207 rz=0.254
let ct_a_c12 = ellipsoid   0.260  1.694   0.547  rx=0.210 ry=0.186 rz=0.215
let ct_a_c13 = ellipsoid   0.216  1.687   0.532  rx=0.233 ry=0.230 rz=0.283
let ct_a_c14 = ellipsoid  -0.076  1.533   0.104  rx=0.234 ry=0.222 rz=0.231
let ct_a_c15 = ellipsoid  -0.180  1.823   0.168  rx=0.234 ry=0.228 rz=0.250
let ct_a_c16 = ellipsoid  -0.230  2.089   0.219  rx=0.296 ry=0.271 rz=0.297
let ct_a_c17 = ellipsoid  -0.249  2.408   0.252  rx=0.220 ry=0.181 rz=0.189
let ct_a_c18 = ellipsoid  -0.214  2.618   0.268  rx=0.186 ry=0.163 rz=0.164
let ct_a_c19 = ellipsoid  -0.217  2.811   0.292  rx=0.277 ry=0.253 rz=0.264
let ct_a_c20 = ellipsoid  -0.252  2.486   0.259  rx=0.290 ry=0.239 rz=0.286
let ct_a_c21 = ellipsoid  -0.298  2.746   0.242  rx=0.183 ry=0.182 rz=0.195
let ct_a_c22 = ellipsoid  -0.406  2.433   0.168  rx=0.264 ry=0.251 rz=0.264
let ct_a_c23 = ellipsoid  -0.475  2.624   0.159  rx=0.296 ry=0.245 rz=0.251
let ct_a_c24 = ellipsoid  -0.226  1.496  -0.041  rx=0.302 ry=0.250 rz=0.313
let ct_a_c25 = ellipsoid  -0.355  1.658  -0.163  rx=0.241 ry=0.233 rz=0.242
